A||N||4||pherder||FYI ... a programmable ECM for the Fiero V6.||||3||
Z||000000||pherder||09-19-2000||04:45 PM||||I am slowly getting "stuff" together for my third Fiero project.  (1st was the HO Quad 4 in my 88 Coupe, 2nd will be rebuild the 2.8 for my 88 GT, and the 3rd project is a TPI 350 V8 in my 88 Formula.)<P>I have been looking at all the different ways to control the TPI system (stock w/stock chip, stock w/aftermarket chip, and totally aftermarket) and have decided to take the plunge and got a Haltech E6GM programmable computer.  It came in today.<P>This computer (ECM) comes with software (DOS based) and a connector to attach to a laptop computer (XT!!!! or above!!).  You have to supply the wiring harness but it is designed to plug into GM wiring harnesses (TBI/TPI).<P>The computer (ECM) is designed to run any engine from 1 to 16 cylinders and controls the fuel and spark.  It can use 1, 2, or 3 bar MAP sensors so turbocharging or supercharging can be used with this ECM.<P>Now for the interesting part.  I was comparing the pinouts from my 88 V6 harness to the E6GM ECMs pinout and all but 2-3 are exactly the same!!!!!!!!!<P>The ones that are different is the MAP and MAT (IAT) sensors and those can be easily moved around.<P>The only thing against this computer (other then the ungodly amount of stuff you can change) is that there is <B>NO</B> provision for a knock sensor.<P> <A HREF="http://www.haltech.com" TARGET=_blank>www.haltech.com</A> <P>Also this is for "racing use only" since there are <B>NO</B> provisions for EGR, canistor purge, or any other emmisions related connections.<P>Paul||||reg||3
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||09-19-2000||06:17 PM||||wow.. I guess I can scrap yet another one of my projects..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <I>(see "Hacking the ECM" thread posted a few days ago)</I><P>how much did this cost?<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 09-19-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||pherder||09-19-2000||06:44 PM||||Retail cost is $695.  Not cheap until you look into the Accell DFI or the others.<P>Accell DFI is ~ $880 <B>PLUS</B> the software for another $200!!<P>There are others but at higher cost.<P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||09-19-2000||08:02 PM||||How do these aftermarket computers sense crankshaft/camshaft position? Do they have their own sensors? Are they preporgrammed with the CPS pulse trains for every engine on the market? Do they only work on distributor engines? Why do you drive on a parkway and park on a driveway?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||RedOktober||09-20-2000||01:47 AM||||Another suggestion is the new TECII from Electromotive <A HREF="http://www.electromotive-inc.com" TARGET=_blank>www.electromotive-inc.com</A> <BR>They have a system that incorporates a knock sensor as well as plug-ins for turbo wastegates as well as NOS solenoids. They use all GM sensors that our V-6's already have. The only thing required is the instalation of the crank trigger. This system is designed to be a piggy back system, they stock system still stays to operate items such as the tach and fan and fuel pump, or it can be used as a stand alone system. Check them out if you are serious about getting some real HP from your turboed V6 on NOS. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater)||||reg||14
